# kiki's guest kernel: the longterm Linux release below with Kata
# Containers' pinned config, plus the
# patches in patches/ and the fragments in fragments/. build.sh and
# source.sh read this file, and the build workflow publishes ghcr.io/ahimsalabs/kiki-kernel:${RELEASE} with
# RELEASE=${KERNEL_VERSION}-${KATA_CONFIG_VERSION}-kiki.${KIKI_REVISION}.
#
# kiki follows Kata for the config and for the kernel's series (README.md has
# the procedure): when a Kata release moves assets.kernel.version to
# another series, or tools/packaging/kernel/kata_config_version, move the
# Kata and Linux values together and reset KIKI_REVISION to 1. Point
# releases within the series come from kernel.org's longterm line, not from
# Kata: KERNEL_VERSION may be newer than the version Kata pins, but is in
# the same series. A new KERNEL_VERSION (with its LINUX_SHA256) resets
# KIKI_REVISION to 1 as well. When anything else that changes the kernel's
# bytes changes (a patch, a fragment, the builder), bump KIKI_REVISION
# alone. A release is published once: installed kikis pin its blobs by
# digest.
KATA_VERSION=4.3.1
KATA_COMMIT=03cf2ec06a5d302f23af811744f6bcea49988bfb
KERNEL_VERSION=6.18.60
KATA_CONFIG_VERSION=208
KIKI_REVISION=1
# linux-${KERNEL_VERSION}.tar.xz from kernel.org, as its sha256sums.asc
# lists it (scripts/bump-kernel.sh checks that file's PGP signature).
LINUX_SHA256=3c2f44ef6acc021a7ad444d9a2e6ec1ef488ff09368793d0004a6080f69d621e
# The build's timestamp (KBUILD_BUILD_TIMESTAMP, the tars' mtimes): the
# Kata commit's committer time, so a rebuild of the same inputs gives the
# same bytes.
SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=1792614631
